# 🧭 Rule Template — Decision Tree

**Purpose:** pick the shape a new rule starts from, then check it against the quality checklist before merging.

## 🧩 Pick a shape

Count the labelled bullets the rule needs, not counting `Read on demand` pointers, then copy the matching file from `rule_template_shapes/`.

- 🪶 **Short shape:** `short.md.template`, for 5 labelled bullets or fewer, with an optional table.
- 🗂️ **Sectioned shape:** `sectioned.md.template`, for more than 5, split into 2 to 4 emoji `##` sections of up to 5 bullets each.
- ✂️ **Too big for either:** past 4 sections or about 110 lines, split it into a parent and 2 or more `<parent>/_<aspect>.md` children, each starting from a shape.
- 🏷️ **Usage header:** an entry-point rule keeps the `applies_to`, `miss_cost` and `loading` lines, and a child file deletes them.

## ✅ Quality Checklist — Both Shapes

This checklist runs past 5 items, since, like numbered steps, it needs every check.

- [ ] **Metadata header:** Lines 1–3 are `<!-- version: X.Y.Z -->`, `<!-- created: YYYY-MM-DD -->`, `<!-- updated: YYYY-MM-DD -->`
  - **New rule:** Version `1.0.0`, with `created` and `updated` both set to today.
  - **Every edit:** Set `updated` to today and bump `version`.
  - **MAJOR:** Guidance reversed or removed.
  - **MINOR:** Guidance added.
  - **PATCH:** Wording or typo fix only.
- [ ] **Usage header:** An entry-point rule has `applies_to`, `miss_cost` and `loading` on lines 4–6, with `loading` matching its folder (`always-on`, `path-scoped` or `on demand`)
- [ ] **Purpose present:** One-line statement at top
- [ ] **H1 emoji header:** File title with emoji
- [ ] **H2 section headers:** All start with emoji (scannability)
- [ ] **Bold keywords:** Bullets open with an emoji, then `**term:**`
- [ ] **One sentence per bullet:** No multi-sentence bullets (use sub-bullets instead)
- [ ] **Five per list:** No list has more than 5 top-level bullets
- [ ] **~100-line limit:** Rules ≤110 lines, split if needed
- [ ] **Trailing newline:** File ends with exactly one `\n`
- [ ] **No H1 duplicates:** Only one `#` header per file
- [ ] **Clear scope:** One concept per file, related patterns grouped
- [ ] **British spelling:** Prose uses -ise and other British forms, while existing identifiers keep their spelling
- [ ] **Enforcement test:** A rule backed by a hook, test or CI check has a test in the repo's `tests/` folder
- [ ] **Contents only when earned:** Add a `## 📋 Contents` section only when the rule has 3 or more real `##` headings, not counting `## 📚 Read on demand`
- [ ] **No Related section:** Link other files only through a `**Read on demand:**` line that says when to read them, never a list of related links
  - **Why:** a rule under `rules/` with no `paths:` setting loads every session, so every line costs always-on context.
